<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Declared in :core:media rather than in :app, because these are this module's own requirements:
  the service class lives here, and a second application module (the roadmap's Wear OS app, Plan 5)
  that depends on this module must get them too without anyone remembering to copy four lines.

  The manifest merger pulls all of it into the application. That is a claim, not a fact, until
  something checks it. `verifyDebugManifest`/`verifyReleaseManifest` (build-logic) read AGP's own
  merged manifest and fail the build if any of these is absent.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!-- The stream comes over the network. -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

  <!-- Spec section 7. FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK is required from API 34 for a service
       whose foregroundServiceType includes mediaPlayback; without it startForeground throws
       SecurityException, and only when the app is actually backgrounded with audio playing. -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MEDIA_PLAYBACK" />

  <!-- dangerous from API 33: needs a runtime grant, and without it the media notification is
       silently not shown rather than reported as an error. -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <application>
    <service
        android:name="app.muplay.media.MuPlaybackService"
        android:exported="true"
        android:foregroundServiceType="mediaPlayback">
      <!-- exported="true" with this action is how a MediaSessionService is discovered by
           Android Auto, Wear, Assistant and the system media controls; there is no way to be
           discovered by them and not be exported.

           No android:permission attribute, and that is deliberate rather than an omission: the
           permission that would express "apps the user trusts with media control" is
           MEDIA_CONTENT_CONTROL, which is signature|privileged and cannot be required of a caller
           by a third-party app. The gate therefore lives where it can be
           expressed: MuPlaybackService.LibraryCallback.onConnect, over ControllerAccessPolicy.

           That gate is code, not this comment. Media3 gates NOTHING by default: the
           MediaSession.Callback default onConnectAsync in media3-session-1.11.0 is
           `Futures.immediateFuture(new AcceptedResultBuilder(session, controllerInfo).build())`,
           read off the bytecode. An earlier version of this comment said Media3 gated through
           onConnect while nothing in this module overrode it, which is the worst of both. See
           ControllerAccessPolicy for what a connected controller can read. -->
      <!-- PLAN 5 TASK 7 - one contiguous block, added to this file by the Android Auto lane.
           Three actions, three different discoverers, and only the first is what Plan 3 needed:

             - androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService -> Media3 MediaControllers (this app's
               UI, and Plan 5 Task 8's :wear app).
             - androidx.media3.session.MediaLibraryService -> Media3 MediaBrowsers asking for the
               browse tree half of the session.
             - android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService -> ANDROID AUTO, Wear OS's bridged media
               surface, and every legacy MediaBrowserCompat client. Auto enumerates media apps by
               THIS action and by no other; an app declaring only Media3's own actions installs,
               runs, passes every test in this repository and never appears in a car. There is no
               error, no log line and no crash - it is simply not in the list. That is why
               verifyDebugManifest/verifyReleaseManifest require it by name.

           None of the three needs a line of code here. Measured off media3-session-1.11.0's own
           bytecode rather than from the docs: MediaLibraryService.onBind returns getServiceBinder()
           for "androidx.media3.session.MediaLibraryService" and otherwise delegates to
           MediaSessionService.onBind, which switches on exactly two strings:
           "androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService" (-> getServiceBinder) and
           "android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" (-> createLegacyControllerInfo, onGetSession,
           then the legacy browser binder) - and returns null for anything else. So the third
           action is bridged by the base class, and declaring it is the entire change.

           exported="true" is what makes any of that possible; Media3 gates the command surface
           behind MediaSession.Callback.onConnect (see ControllerAccessPolicy). -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService" />
        <action android:name="androidx.media3.session.MediaLibraryService" />
        <action android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" />
      </intent-filter>
      <!-- PLAN 5 TASK 6 - one contiguous block, added by the voice-and-search lane.
           "Hey Google, play <something> on MuPlay" when nothing is connected yet: the Assistant
           starts this service with this action and a SearchManager.QUERY extra, and
           MuPlaybackService.onStartCommand answers it. With the app already connected the same
           spoken request arrives instead as a legacy playFromSearch, which Media3 turns into
           MediaLibrarySession.Callback.onSetMediaItems with a query on the item's request metadata;
           both reach MuPlayLibraryCallback.spokenQueue, which is the one place that decides what a
           spoken query plays.

           A SEPARATE intent-filter and not a fourth action in the one above, deliberately: the
           three actions above are all bound by Media3's own onBind and take no category, while this
           one is a started-service intent that needs DEFAULT to be resolvable by the Assistant.
           Merging them would make the service claim to answer MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH on a bind,
           which it does not.

           ConventionTest's `a declared play-from-search filter must have a handler and a gate
           entry` holds this filter, that handler and the AUTOMOTIVE_DECLARATIONS entry to a single
           answer, so no two of the three can land without the third. -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.media.action.MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
      </intent-filter>
    </service>
  </application>
</manifest>
